sevenz/ - 7-Zip SDK decoder cores for ZIP-style 7z support.
==========================================================

Source
------
7-Zip SDK (ip7z/7zip, `main` branch), PUBLIC DOMAIN (Igor Pavlov),
fetched as raw files and copied verbatim. `LICENSE` is the SDK's
DOC/7zC.txt (LZMA SDK public-domain statement).

Only the *codec cores* are vendored - NOT the container:
`7zArcIn.c`, `7zDec.c`, `7zIn.c`, `7zItem.c` are deliberately NOT
taken (they drag in the SDK allocator/stream machinery); the .7z
container is parsed by our own clean-room `SevenZDecode.cpp` over
these cores, same pattern as `XzDecode`/`LzmaDecode`.

Compiled in (see XacRett.dsp "sevenz" group):
  Aes.c Aes.h Sha256.c Sha256.h   AES-256-CBC + SHA-256
  Bcj2.c Bcj2.h                   BCJ2 decoder
  Bra.c Bra.h Bra86.c             branch converters (Bra.c has no X86;
                                  X86 lives in Bra86.c)
  CpuArch.c CpuArch.h             CPU feature detection
  Delta.c Delta.h                 Delta (coder 03)
  Ppmd7.c Ppmd7.h Ppmd7Dec.c
  Ppmd.h                          PPMdH (coder 04)
Headers only: 7zTypes.h 7zWindows.h Compiler.h Precomp.h
              RotateDefs.h

Not compiled: 7zArcIn.c 7zDec.c 7zIn.c 7zItem.c 7zAlloc.c
              Bcj2Enc.c BraIA64.c Bra86 asm variants, AesOpt.c
              Sha256Opt.c CpuArch asm, and the whole CPP/ tree.

VC6 port shims (2 files, both REAL upstream-gate bugs)
-----------------------------------------------------
Upstream enables hardware paths on a bare `_MSC_VER` test:

  Sha256.c:  `#if ... defined(_MSC_VER) && (_MSC_VER >= 1200)`
  Aes.c:     `#elif defined(_MSC_VER)`

**VC6 reports _MSC_VER == 1200**, so BOTH tests wrongly match, and
the code then references assembly-only symbols
(`Sha256_UpdateBlocks_HW`, `AesCbc_{Encode,Decode}_HW`,
`AesCtr_Code_HW`, `AesCbc_Decode_HW_256`, `AesCtr_Code_HW_256`,
`CPU_IsSupported_{SHA,SSSE3,AES,VAES_AVX2}`) that only exist in
.asm / hardware-only builds -> LNK2001 at link. We narrowed both to
`_MSC_VER > 1200`, which keeps the pure-C paths. Correct for a
1998-era target: no SHA-NI / AES-NI instructions exist.

A second, sharper trap of the same family: `Sha256_Init` stores NULL
into `p->v.vars.func_UpdateBlocks` in the `#else` (no-HW) branch, so
a caller MUST NOT be told to call `Sha256Prepare()`-driven dispatch
expecting a non-null pointer - the direct-call path
(`SHA256_UPDATE_BLOCKS(p)` macro) is what actually runs.

Verification (VC6, in-tree harness `mk7ztest.bat`)
--------------------------------------------------
o SHA-256: 3 NIST vectors exact - "abc", empty, 'a'x1000.
o AES-256-CBC: NIST SP800-38A F.2.5 exact, both directions;
  NIST F.1.5 (ECB, 1 block) exact.
o Delta: output matches a from-spec reference model for delta 1..16.
  NOTE Delta is NOT self-inverse across a single call pair - the
  decoder must start from the same zero state the encoder started
  from (the encoder's FINAL state is the trailing `delta` bytes and
  is NOT the decoder's initial state).
o Bra x86 (ConvSt_X86_Enc/Dec): encode->decode restores input.

Required call order / buffer layout (cost real debugging time)
------------------------------------------------------------
o `Sha256Prepare()` once before any `Sha256_Init`.
o AES: key schedule goes at `Aes()+4`, IV at `Aes()+0`; buffer is
  `UInt32 [AES_NUM_IVMRK_WORDS + 3]`, all 16-byte aligned.
  Decrypt needs `Aes_SetKey_Dec`, encrypt `Aes_SetKey_Enc`.
  (`AesCbc_Init` fills ONLY the IV - it does not touch the schedule.)

Container side (clean-room): SevenZDecode.cpp / SevenZCore.cpp
-----------------------------------------------------------
The .7z container is OUR parser; only the codec cores above are
vendored.  `SevenZDecode.cpp` walks PackInfo / UnPackInfo /
SubStreamsInfo / FilesInfo and builds a coder graph; `SevenZCore.cpp`
wraps the SDK cores in a plain-byte API.

TU split is MANDATORY: `sevenz/7zTypes.h` and `lzma/7zTypes.h` share
the include guard `ZIP7_7Z_TYPES_H`.  One TU cannot include both (they
define the same ISzAlloc/ISzAllocPtr differently).  So:
  SevenZCore.cpp  - sevenz/* only (AES, SHA-256, Bcj2, Bra, Delta, Ppmd7)
  SevenZDecode.cpp - lzma/* + zlib + libbzip2 + zstd, via SevenZCore.h

TRAPS found while wiring the cores up (all cost real debugging time)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
IByteIn is a DIRECT-CALL interface in this SDK vintage, not the old
Vtbl one:
    Z7_C_IFACE_DECL (IByteIn) { Byte (*Read)(IByteInPtr p); };
    #define IByteIn_Read(p) (p)->Read(p)
So `p->Stream` is a plain `const IByteIn *`, and passing a buffer
descriptor struct cast to `IByteInPtr` makes the decoder call the
buffer's first member as a FUNCTION POINTER -> 0xC0000005, with no
RTC report and identical /Od and /O2.  The working shape is to embed
`IByteIn` as the FIRST member of the wrapper struct and point
`CPpmd7_RangeDec::Stream` at it:
    struct SzcPpmdIn { IByteIn in; const BYTE* buf; DWORD len,pos; bool err; };
    model.rc.dec.Stream = (IByteInPtr)&in;      // valid: in is first
A 7z PPMd stream has NO end marker.  The encoder only FLUSHES the
range coder after the last symbol, so `Code` ends at whatever the
flush produced, `Ppmd7z_RangeDec_IsFinishedOK` is false, and the
remaining input is renormalisation.  Demanding PPMD7_SYM_END (or a
clean range coder, or `pos == len`) rejects every valid archive.
Only two checks are honest: exact output length, and that the range
coder never had to read past the packed stream.  Entry CRCs (and the
header CRC for an encoded header) cover the bytes themselves.

7z ZSTD coder: method id `04F71101`, properties are 5 bytes
`[zstd_major, zstd_minor, level, 0, 0]` (3-byte historic form also
legal).  There is NO dictionary or window size in the properties -
those live in the zstd frame header - so the payload is one plain
zstd frame, exactly like the ZIP method 93 payload.  Do NOT refuse on
a properties version newer than our vendored zstd: the frame format is
forward compatible, so only the output length is checked.  Verified
against the coder record in a real 7-Zip 19.00+zstd archive
(workarea root `7z-zstd.7z`, `Method = BCJ 04F71101`).

7zAES: what actually has to be true (three bugs, all silent)
----------------------------------------------------------
1. The KDF hashes the password as **UTF-16LE**, never the raw bytes
   of the ANSI string the UI hands us.  7-Zip converts its ANSI input
   with CP_ACP and hashes the UTF-16 form, so hashing `strlen()`
   bytes produces a perfectly well-formed key that decrypts nothing.
   (The key is then *correct-looking*: same length, same KDF, no
   error anywhere - the only symptom is "aes check failed".)  The
   conversion therefore belongs at the CALL SITE (SevenZDecode.cpp,
   `MultiByteToWideChar(CP_ACP, MB_PRECOMPOSED, ...)`), and
   `SevenZAesDeriveKey` takes `(const BYTE*, size_t)`.
2. `SevenZAesDecryptCbc` works IN PLACE, and `dst` in runCoder is a
   freshly `new`ed, uninitialised output buffer - the ciphertext must
   be memcpy'd out of `src[0]` first.  Without it you decrypt
   whatever heap garbage was there.
3. A 7zAES payload carries **NO embedded digest**.  The encoder
   rounds the plaintext up to a 16-byte boundary with ZEROS (p7zip's
   "AES filters need 16-bytes alignment" rule), and the coder outSize
   in the header is that padded size.  So the tail of the ciphertext
   is padding, not a SHA-256 of anything, and `IsFinishedOK`-style
   checks over the tail are meaningless.  Verified by decrypting
   m93_aes.7z with python-zstd-independent tooling: 310565 real bytes
   + 11 zero bytes of padding = the recorded 310576.  Integrity comes
   from the entry CRCs instead (Extract deletes the output on
   mismatch), which is what py7zr and our ZIP WinZip-AES path do.

With all three fixed the derived key matches py7zr's `calculate_key`
bit for bit, and both encrypted fixtures (m93_aes.7z / "secret" and
aes4.7z / "testpw") extract byte-exact.  A wrong password fails
loudly one stage later ("lzma2 data"), never silently.
